# Agent

An **agent** is whatever performs cognitive operations: it takes an input,
applies an operation such as induction, evaluation or revision, and returns a
result that something else can use. The word is in general use; the
definition by operations is this project's. It makes the agent a capability
and not a particular machine: a person, a program and a language model are
all agents to the extent that they can perform the operations on the list.

## Description

### The thesis: the operations are what an agent is

The seventeen operations catalogued as [[cognitive-operation]] were first
listed as things a language model does. Read the other way round, the list is
a definition. Every item on it is a capability of an agent, and nothing else
in the vault has those capabilities: a card holds knowledge, a query computes,
a revision records. Only an agent infers, explains, compares, condenses,
judges and corrects.

So an agent is defined here by one property: it can perform the operations in
a list. Two consequences follow.

- **The list is open.** Seventeen is where the discussion stopped. Whoever
  uses the agent can add an operation, and an agent that could perform the
  seventeen can perform the eighteenth without being rebuilt, provided the
  operation is given to it as a definition and not built into it.
- **The generic case comes first.** Before any specialised agent there is the
  plain one that can perform every operation and has no speciality. Other
  agents are that one plus a restriction or an addition.

### Three senses kept apart

The word is used in three ways, and they are different things.

| Sense | What it names | Where it appears |
|---|---|---|
| The role | whoever stands at the step where a result is interpreted and a change proposed | "a human or agent" in the designs for executable documents |
| The capability | being able to perform the operations in a list | this card's definition |
| The construct | an element of a language: a type whose values can be asked to perform an operation | the design for the query language |

**The role.** In every loop the discussion draws there is a step that is not
mechanical: someone reads the output and decides what it means and what to
change. The discussion writes "human or agent" at that step each time and
means it. A person adding axioms to a theory can be replaced by a program that
does the same, so the role does not require a human. Who fills it, and who
approves what it proposes, is the subject of [[human-in-the-loop]].

**The capability.** This is the sense defined above. It says what the
occupant of the role must be able to do, whoever it is.

**The construct.** If operations are what an agent does, a language that
works with agents needs the agent as one of its elements, in the way it has
numbers and records. The natural shape is a type with a capability attached,
a trait in the language's terms: a value of the type can be asked to perform
any operation, and the answer comes back typed. The operations themselves stay
outside the type, as data, which is what keeps the list open.

### What an agent is not

- **Not the model.** A language model is one thing that can fill the role.
  The agent is the model together with its instructions, the operations it is
  given and the tools it may call.
- **Not a function.** A function has one signature. An agent accepts any
  operation, including ones defined after it was.
- **Not trusted by default.** Asking an agent to deduce is not a checked
  deduction. Each result is owed its support, which is why every operation is
  a [[typed-transformation]].
- **Not self-acting.** In the designs of this vault an agent proposes. What
  is integrated and what is run are separate decisions.

### Instructions as documents

Today an agent's instructions are usually a natural-language file read at the
start of a session. The essay behind this project asks what happens when some
of those instructions become executable or verifiable contracts. This vault's
answer is one step further: the instructions are a card in the same system as
the knowledge the agent works on, so that they can be linked, queried and
revised like anything else, and so that the agent can read a description of
itself. That specification is the [[hmd-agent]].

## Relations

- It is defined by being able to perform each [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: uses}, and any operation added to that list.
- Its sub-specification in this project is the [[hmd-agent]] {relation: generalizes}: an agent defined as a card.
- A [[reflective-agent]] {relation: generalizes} is an agent that uses a model of itself to revise itself.
- It fills the role that [[human-in-the-loop]] {relation: contrasts} reserves a part of for a person.
- Its results are each a [[typed-transformation]] {relation: uses}, with support attached.
- It is the occupant of the interpreting step in the [[hmd-feedback-loop]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is what turns the [[reasoning-cycle]] {relation: uses}: none of the cycle's steps performs itself.
- It bears on the question [[ai-oriented-language]] {relation: answers}: such a language has the agent as one of its elements.
- It bears on the question [[ai-support-in-hql]] {relation: answers}.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[hmd-agent]].** The sub-specification: an agent whose instructions are
  the text of a card and whose operations are other cards. It is the form in
  which this project builds agents.
- **[[cognitive-operation]].** The catalogue of seventeen operations with
  their inputs and outputs. Seen from this card it is the agent's interface.
- **[[reflective-agent]].** An agent that models its own reasoning and uses
  the model to change its rules. Every reflective agent is an agent; an agent
  need not be reflective.
- **[[human-in-the-loop]].** The arrangement in which a person interprets
  results and approves changes. It is the same role as the agent's, held by a
  person at the points where a decision must not be automatic.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[agent]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[agent]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

The agent cards that exist, in the agents vault:

```hql
source("agents").cards | map(c => {agent: c.name, title: c.title})
```

Future lens that applies to this card: [[perform-operation]] gives the third
sense, the construct, as a trait and a result type.

